it's an update to a story we first  Brought You in September Helping Seniors  get access to food and after our story  aired one local non-profit reached out  to help in hopes of Bridging the Gap  when it comes to seniors and food  insecurity  residents at the Royal mobile home park  in the Northeast Valley can now enjoy a  Thanksgiving meal this holiday season  now kokomora is the founder of a local  non-profit called the Center for Women  and families abroad she says her  non-profit has limited resources but  helps in any way she can this is from my  personal experience I was a single  mother and we I went through some  struggles in the past and I do  understand  how important to help others so I  decided to do this with 80 bags of food  filled with canned goods and boxed meals  Community manager Virginia buzzart says  this will help feed 50 to 60 struggling  families it's really hard I have one  tenant that he only gets like 900 a  month  and it's very hard for him and I try to  help as much as I can but I can only do  so much Mae Simpson first reached out to  us for help back in September she says  she is overwhelmed with the quick  response and generosity from those  willing to help when I came in and saw  how much food there was I was actually  blown away it was tremendous you know  suddenly I did not expect  and as a way to give back Channel 8  staff did help bag and distribute the  food as well and Kimura says she hopes  to help others in the community who need  help accessing food
 

